<article class="env-lemma" id="0AH6">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0AH6">Lemma <span data-tag="0AH6">29.42.10</span>.</a> Suppose given a commutative diagram of schemes </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix{ X \ar[rr]_ h \ar[rd]_ f &  &  Y \ar[ld]^ g \\  &  S }  \]
</div>
  <p> Assume </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$X \to S$ is a universally closed (for example proper) morphism, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$Y \to S$ is separated and locally of finite type. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
  <p> Then the scheme theoretic image $Z \subset Y$ of $h$ is proper over $S$ and $X \to Z$ is surjective. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      The scheme theoretic image of $h$ is constructed in Section <a href="/tag/01R5" data-tag="01R5">29.6</a>. Since $f$ is quasi-compact (Lemma <a href="/tag/04XU" data-tag="04XU">29.42.8</a>) we find that $h$ is quasi-compact (Schemes, Lemma <a href="/tag/03GI" data-tag="03GI">26.21.14</a>). Hence $h(X) \subset Z$ is dense (Lemma <a href="/tag/01R8" data-tag="01R8">29.6.3</a>). On the other hand $h(X)$ is closed in $Y$ (Lemma <a href="/tag/01W6" data-tag="01W6">29.42.7</a>) hence $X \to Z$ is surjective. Thus $Z \to S$ is a proper (Lemma <a href="/tag/03GN" data-tag="03GN">29.42.9</a>). 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>